an that was what i'm just  repeating in conversation that i had  with with somebody who wanted to talk to  me about about the chaos in the airports  so you put this all on the industry do  you think the government could do  anything about this what would you do  well i think that if i was transport  minister i would be sitting down with  the airlines and saying what is the plan  how are you going to get through this  and what can we do to help you you know  because we need to make sure that we  have a we have a transporter industry  that works properly that are that our  airports work as efficiently as possible  you can't have people waiting for hours  for baggage or indeed people waiting for  hours to get through immigration um or  their flights being cancelled whilst  they're sitting in the airport it's not  good enough so yes i think that it's  right for the minister to bring people  in and challenge them and find out how  they're going to ensure that this  doesn't happen people sacrifice in order  to save enough money to be able to go on  holiday with their family and for that  holiday to be cancelled at the last  minute is simply not acceptable in this  day and age you say you want a transport  system that works what about the rail  system we're facing huge strikes next  week three days of strikes but a whole  week of disruption  do you back the strikers  i think that what they should be doing  is they should be sitting down with the  employers and with government and  discussing  how they're going to get through this we  have very low paid cleaners for example  on the trains who are finding it very  difficult to meet their bills now that  will happen up and down the country in  all sorts of different industries in the  near future because of the cost of  living crisis because of inflation so we  need to make sure that we are not  encouraging strikes we are encouraging  people to talk and and what we have is a  government that is standing back and  saying we're not talking to anybody we  want to just point the finger at labor  and blame labor as if it's our fault not  not encouraging strikes but supporting  strikers do you support those who are  going to walk out next week i don't want  them to walk out next week but no no one  wants them to know that that's why you  know so i'm not supporting them to walk  out next week i want you to attack them  he takes two no no no no no i think that  it takes two to tango what we need is we  need the unions and the employers with  government because it's you know very  important than the amount of government  money that goes into our our rail  industry we need those them to sit down  and actually think about how they are  going to probably said that so would you  say to those strikers do not go on  strike i would say that any strike is a  failure what we need to be doing is we  need to be able to find a way through it  together and we need to set that as a  precedent because this is going to be a  crisis that will apply to many  industries in the near future and  responsible government would be involved  i mean you know one here is that they  are bragging about the fact they haven't  spoken to the union since march why not  but it feels like you don't want to take  a tough position on this i mean normally  wouldn't a labour party be very happy to  to stand up for strikers and say we back  them in taking this action  we want to make sure that we have a  transport system that works properly it  does not work properly when relations  have broken down so much that people are  threatening to go on strike we need to  make sure that our rail industry works  properly and we cannot have a government  that simply thinks that their job is to  score political points they need to be  in the weeds with the employers and the  unions and the workers and say how are  we going to deal with this how are we  given that it's going to be 11 inflation  how do we make sure that you know the  lady who who cleans the the carriages  lose you know is getting paid properly  so that she can meet her bills  it sounds like you want to put this all  on the government but but  i i'm interested to know why you won't  stick your neck out on a position on on  backing the strikers or not is the  labour party just scared of offending  some sections of society no i'm taking  an overview so during the pandemic  because so many of the railways were  dependent obviously on the fairs and  people weren't traveling on the trains  it means that many of the railway  companies  are don't have sufficient income they  are using that much as i was saying  about british airways you know i think  that they are they're over steering in  the wrong direction they're trying to  cut back on routes they're trying to cut  back on employees and they're not  prepared to increase wages that then  means that you have to take a strategic  view of this which is what the job of  government is the government should be  taking a strategic view of what is what  is best for all of us when it comes to  our railway system and not spending time  having a pop at the labour party okay  let's move on to the economy you  mentioned 11 inflation looming as the  bank of england pointed out yesterday  so people are facing an unprecedented  cost of living squeeze what can be done  about it  we think that there are a number of  things that can be done and we've we've  obviously we talked about the um the the  levy that has that has happened the  windfall tax um uh on on energy energy  companies i mean the government and the  government has started to do that and  it's it's committed to going further  than new suggestions yeah although it  doesn't really  there are all kinds of technical  problems with it i mean for example you  know if someone was to own four homes um  then they would get they would get that  lump sum you know in every single home  that they had i mean these sorts of  things are a problem um there are there  ar