oing to get to  replace him because you know that we  have a government who is committed as  far as i can see to pushing things as  much as they can seeing what they can  get away with and believing themselves  to be above the law so what do you think  should happen to the role do you think  it should change at all should it be we  should be decided by mps generally in  parliament and an independent what do  you think should happen i don't think  this is going to get fixed by having a  new ethics advisor i think the only way  that we're going to get number 10 honest  is to get a new prime minister  well  no big surprise for labor to call for a  different prime minister  but it comes but the culture comes from  the top when you have a prime minister  who thinks it's fine to do whatever you  like and somehow or other rules don't  apply to them you then get ministers who  start aping it ministers who think that  they can behave in this way as well keep  pushing things along that's what they're  doing that's what this government is  about you know but it shouldn't be like  that we're a democracy the law is  important you should not be just  thinking you can do whatever you like  and somehow or other it'll be fine and  um we should talk about what you wanted  to talk about today there's so many  other issues that i i did want to put to  you uh which were of great interest to  everybody but you have written a letter  to boris johnson today about support for  rape survivors tell us what you're  asking  along with 98 um labor women mps we're  really worried about the changes that  are being proposed to  what can be disclosed to the defendant  in a rape case and they're watering down  the rules as far as we can see on the  rules on whether or not um if a woman  goes to get therapy and there are notes  made we're really worried that they're  making it easier for those notes to be  disclosed to the defendant and given  that only like 1.5 percent of rapes  actually end up going